      "title": "tensor \u2014 one element, one lattice",
      "description": "Three 6\u00d76 lattices, one per way of supplying values: live, static, and baked.",
      "markdown": "# `tensor`\n\nEach grid below is **one element**, not 36.\n\n**Left \u2014 `valueExpr`, live.** One expression, evaluated per cell with `row` and `col` bound. Row $i$ is a softmax over $s_{ij} = e^{-|i-j|\\cdot k}$, so every row sums to 1. Drag **sharpness** and watch it go from uniform to near-diagonal.\n\n**Middle \u2014 `values`, static.** Literal numbers, built once, **zero** per-frame cost: a causal mask, the shape that makes parallel training legal.\n\n**Right \u2014 `dataTable`, baked.** Also one `valueExpr`, but it reads a table in the scene's `data` block:\n\n```\ndataTable('attn', row, concat('w', col))\n```\n\nThis is how a real lesson supplies numbers \u2014 the weights come from a model and are baked into the JSON, so nothing is invented at render time and every value is reproducible offline. This head attends mostly to the **previous token**, partly to **position 0**, and never to the future.\n\nCell geometry is arithmetic on `shape`, not expressions, so nothing is re-evaluated for positions that never move.",
